WEBVTT
TITLE Graph Representations — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Here is one undirected graph: six vertices, and seven edges. Nothing about it is a choice yet.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The first register is a square grid: one row and one column per vertex, and a 1 where an edge runs.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The second register is a card per vertex, listing only that vertex neighbours.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The third register is a list of edges: seven pairs. Three registers, one graph.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Start with an empty six by six grid and number the rows and columns by the same vertex order.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The edge A-B sets cell A,B and cell B,A. One edge, two cells.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Seven edges later, fourteen cells are set and the grid is mirror-symmetric about its diagonal.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The diagonal stays zero because a simple graph has no loop. A loop would put a 1 on the diagonal.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Add each row and you get the degree of that vertex: 2, 2, 4, 2, 2, 2.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Add the whole grid instead and you get fourteen, which is twice the number of edges.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
So the degrees must add to twice the edge count. This is the handshake lemma, read off the grid.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The list 2, 2, 4, 2, 2, 2 is the degree sequence. It is not the graph.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The list keeps one head per vertex, then a chain of that vertex neighbours.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
C appears on four chains because C has four neighbours; A appears on two.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
For a directed graph each edge appears once, so the chains hold m entries, not two m.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Both copies of an edge are called twins, and setting a twin pointer in every entry costs Theta(n + m).

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The matrix always costs n squared cells, whether the graph is empty or complete.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The list costs a head per vertex and eight bytes per entry: here 24 bytes plus 112.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
At a thousand vertices and two thousand edges the list needs 36,000 bytes against 125,000 for a packed matrix.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Make the graph dense and the order reverses: 6,404,000 bytes of list against 125,000 of packed matrix.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Is A joined to D? The matrix reads one cell and answers in constant time.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The list walks A chain instead: two entries here, but up to the degree of A.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Walking the whole graph on a matrix reads all 36 cells; on a list it takes 6 heads plus 14 entries.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
So DFS is Theta(n squared) on a matrix and Theta(n + m) on a list, and GATE has asked exactly that.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Multiply the matrix by itself. Entry i,j of A squared counts the walks of length two from i to j.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The diagonal of A squared is the degree sequence: 2, 2, 4, 2, 2, 2.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Off the diagonal it counts common neighbours: A and C share B, so the entry is 1.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Add all of A squared and you get every walk of length two: 36 of them here.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The diagonal of A cubed counts closed walks of length three: two at each corner of a triangle, zero elsewhere.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The trace adds the diagonal: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6. One triangle contributed all six.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Six is three corners times two directions: that is why the divisor is six and not three.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Powers of A answer structural questions, so the matrix is more than a storage format.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
The incidence matrix has a row per vertex and a column per edge: six rows, seven columns.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Every column holds exactly two ones, one at each end of that edge, and the column sums are all 2.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Row sums are the degrees again. Over GF(2) the rank is n minus the number of components.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
That nullity is the number of independent cycles: two here, a triangle and a four-cycle.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Four questions pick the structure: how dense, which query, is memory tight, do you need powers of A.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Now the trap. These two graphs have the same degree sequence: 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
So a degree sequence never settles a graph question. Triangle counts, or A cubed, separate them.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
A representation never changes the graph. It only decides what is cheap, and every claim here was computed on this page.

